From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 22:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath10k: restart the firmware when a SET_KEY install
 times out

ath10k_install_key() sends WMI_VDEV_INSTALL_KEY_CMDID and waits 3 s for
the firmware to acknowledge it.  If the firmware does not answer, the
function returns -ETIMEDOUT, every caller logs it, and the driver carries
on talking to a firmware whose crypto state is now unknown.

On WCN3990 (msm8998, Pixel 2 XL) this is not recoverable when it happens
on the SET_KEY leg: after one unacknowledged key command the firmware
kept doing 802.11 management on its own - eight further associations all
completed with status=0 - but returned a DROP completion for every data
frame, so no EAPOL frame ever left the chip and the interface stayed
unusable until reboot.  The firmware never crashed, so no existing
recovery trigger fired.  Ask for a firmware restart instead, the same
way ath10k_wmi_cmd_send() treats a WMI command it cannot hand to the
firmware within 3 s.

Deliberately do not escalate on the DISABLE_KEY leg.  That leg runs
during disconnect, roam and the pre-suspend teardown cfg80211 performs
for drivers suspending without WoWLAN, which is the firmware's busiest
control-path moment and the one where WCN3990 routinely misses the 3 s
deadline (the same -110 was reported upstream against Cisco APs during
FT roams and never resolved).  The peer is about to be deleted and the
vdev stopped regardless, so an unacknowledged key removal costs nothing
extra - while a firmware restart here turns a bounded 3 s stall into a
measured 90 s outage: the restart work contends with the in-flight
teardown on conf_mutex, sets ATH10K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH which fails the
remaining teardown with -ESHUTDOWN, and mac80211's reconfig then holds
rtnl_lock across a full firmware reboot.  Measured on a Pixel 2 XL,
that restart fired on nearly every wake from system suspend, when
wpa_supplicant roams from the 2.4 GHz to the 5 GHz BSS of the same AP
about 35 s after reconnecting.  Neither ath11k nor ath12k restarts the
firmware on any key install timeout.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c | 17 ++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c
index 4db75dc91eb7..372851734d95 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/mac.c
@@ -326,8 +326,23 @@ static int ath10k_install_key(struct ath10k_vif *arvif,
 		return ret;
 
 	time_left = wait_for_completion_timeout(&ar->install_key_done, 3 * HZ);
-	if (time_left == 0)
+	if (time_left == 0) {
+		/* An unacknowledged SET_KEY leaves a link that is meant to
+		 * stay up with unusable crypto state; restarting the firmware
+		 * costs one reconnect instead of a dead interface.  An
+		 * unacknowledged DISABLE_KEY is the teardown path: the peer
+		 * is about to be deleted and the vdev stopped regardless, and
+		 * a restart here converts a bounded 3 s stall into a
+		 * multi-second outage while mac80211 reconfigures under
+		 * rtnl_lock.
+		 */
+		if (cmd == SET_KEY) {
+			ath10k_warn(ar, "vdev %i install key timed out, restarting hardware\n",
+				    arvif->vdev_id);
+			ath10k_core_start_recovery(ar);
+		}
 		return -ETIMEDOUT;
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
